A||||1||Richard Parnell||Two questions Trans and throttle cable||||5||
Z||000000||Richard Parnell||02-25-2000||07:21 PM||||I have a couple of things I'm curious about.  Does the 5 speed manual tranny on the Fieros have the same amount of power applied to both axles (wheels) like positraction?<BR>The other item I asked about the other day in another thread but never got an answer probably because I "barged" into another throttle question.  (That was rude of me).<BR>Not long ago I had a friend push the gas pedal to the floor (w/engine off) while I watched the throttle move on the engine.  I noticed that I could still move the throttle a least another inch or so even with the gas pedal all the way down.  Does this mean my cable is stretched or is this normal?  It seems that I would get more gas in if the throttle went all the way to the end of it's throw?  Am I wrong on this?  Has anyone else ever noticed this?||||reg||5
Z||000001||DRH||02-28-2000||12:01 PM||||Richard,<P>The transaxle is not positive traction. It uses a spider gear differential just like most cars. There was a thread about a month ago that seems to come to the conclusion it ACTS like positive traction because of the drive train layout (traverse/midengine).<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/002089.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/002089.html</A> <P>The throttle plate should rotate fully open when the pedal is pushed to the floor. Look inside the throttle body to confirm this. If it doesn't, I'm not sure how to adjust...<P>David.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 02-28-2000).]||||reg||1
